[{:db/id "q-353168/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "74c9f568-f128-534b-bb54-d2ce9c02fb0e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353168/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "93681da3-e0dc-54e0-a993-2d8efbe4be99" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353168/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-353168/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353168" :question/id #uuid "29e706f9-8ca4-554a-849d-64238c74c633" :question/math-academy-id "q-353168" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}=\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 4}-\\frac{3x}{3\\cdot 5}+\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{2}}{4\\cdot 6}-\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{3}}{5\\cdot 7}+\\frac{{3}^{4}{x}^{4}}{6\\cdot 8}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}{(n+2)(n+4)}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(3x)^{n+1}}{(n+3)(n+5)}\\cdot \\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(-1)^{n}(3x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-3x\\,(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+2)(n+4)}{(n+3)(n+5)} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-353168/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72ee4476-9ccd-5e3a-a1aa-efe576b4d55e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353168"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-353156/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "2fad246b-2b64-5394-a6fd-f4596f508fce" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353156/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "9edd6f8e-cd66-5b0d-945e-e5ba15643f75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-353156/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "0c477079-520f-5c6a-965f-fc1cd1ca7074" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-353156/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-353156/field-1/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-353156/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-353156" :question/id #uuid "abfc2567-19f5-537f-b5c4-a88a68c6d417" :question/math-academy-id "q-353156" :question/problem "Find the radius of convergence $R$ of the following series:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}=\\frac{x}{{4}^{2}}+\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{3}^{2}{x}^{3}}{{6}^{2}}+\\frac{{3}^{3}{x}^{4}}{{7}^{2}}+⋯\n$$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To apply the ratio test, we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{3}^{n+1}{x}^{n+2}}{(n+5)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{{3}^{n}{x}^{n+1}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{3x(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}}| \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(n+4)^{2}}{(n+5)^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{(1+\\frac{4}{n})}^{2}}{{(1+\\frac{5}{n})}^{2}} \\\\  & =3|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if \n\n$$\n3|x|<1\\,⇒\\,|x|<\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{3}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-353156/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "72ee4476-9ccd-5e3a-a1aa-efe576b4d55e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-353156"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection/fe472ff3ff70f2b497884848b58a844440c390a3db40d74604ce5e927b13e9a8" :answer/id #uuid "2125cefa-33d0-5f43-889a-5861d0b8f9e0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection/d41285927bfe53c04a09af44c9b458e37b7d9d23c8d0fa4d5c42e910751265fc" :answer/id #uuid "cb080cab-cde8-525f-8f38-a8add49c5c57" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=\\sqrt{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection/bcb3a13c00508924772cd99f87896054b10639a22a6eee40c385ea4e630fbe24" :answer/id #uuid "1f07d7f5-a5ed-50c3-8480-0bce5b78e212" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection/8beee86ea931f536f05e8808030c4cf83bdbbc410eb7a15e1e5f39fe305fdc6e" :answer/id #uuid "fadea26e-851e-5ba0-bc49-b4d5856d2365" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection/f970a95b2ce9bd563f661045c30e6a55f907c688f1d23c77f97fa3e63a89ac5c" :answer/id #uuid "0c67dd4e-ec14-5cc8-a174-810cf09028c5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "R=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-9896/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "7f77bb63-4f82-50af-8565-6ba26258e618" :answer-field/k